[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) holds <ProblemCount ticket={4471} /> reports about dates, merged as one
issue because their subject lines looked alike ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
Read what each report shows, and where it came from, and they are three problems. Each has its own
ticket now.

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser does not close #4471. The hint and one-day reports stay broken after that fix.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## The three problems

### `parser`: day and month swap: <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" /> reports · <a href={tickets.parser}>ticket #1</a>

The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so
7 April is stored as 4 July. Only days 1 to 12 can swap, so most bookings look fine and the rest land
months away. These are reports from day-first tenants by app, by email, or through a `booking-v2` form,
counted as the desk files them by tenant, so the one-day moves below are in this count too.

### `hint`: the form tells people to type month first: <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" /> reports · <a href={tickets.hint}>ticket #2</a>

`booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field and reads what is typed the tenant's way, day
first. People do what the hint says and get a swapped date before the parser sees it. These are the
reports that came through the form at the `booking-v3` tenants. The same tenants' app and email
reports are `parser`. Fix the parser alone and these stay broken.

### `one-day move`: the date moves by one day, it does not swap: <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="one-day move" /> reports · <a href={oneDay}>ticket #3</a>

The desk read these as a one-day move already (`desk-notes.json`), and the bodies say so: Tuesday
became Monday, Lisbon and Toronto saw different days for one meeting, June was one day off, an invite
came before its booking. A swap cannot do that. It looks like time zone or daylight-saving handling.
The desk counts them under `parser` by tenant, and so does this page; the parser fix will not fix
them, so they have their own ticket.

## How the split is made

- One id, one report: the export repeats some reports, so they are counted once (`uniqueById`).
- The tenant decides the cause, not intake's tag (`causes.ts`). Intake guesses from the reporter's
  browser and is wrong in both directions; the "tagged as" column shows by how much.
- Counts are the desk's own view: `groupByCause` and `readAs` from `causes.ts`, as `CauseTable`
  uses them (`split.ts`).
- The desk's own notes decide the one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`).
- Every number on this page is computed from `reports-4471.json` when the page renders.

## Lessons

- A matching subject line says two people are unhappy about the same thing, not that one bug did it.
  Read the body and the way in before you merge.
- When a note on the desk contradicts the table, the table is wrong: give the note its own row.

